1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,580
So we sum those up and we get n plus 1 of these.

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:03,660
All right?

3
00:00:04,420 --> 00:00:11,020
So, and then we just go and solve for q. And we have q is equal to a over b times n plus 1.

4
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:17,620
And then capital Q is just going to be equal to little q times what?

5
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:20,060
N. N. Right?

6
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:23,760
So then an over b times n plus 1.

7
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:26,540
Okay. Right.

8
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:33,540
And if you take a look at this, this is an equation that your moderate democratic economist is going

9
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:34,100
to like much more.

10
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:37,880
Because this says no, two firms is not as good as an infinite number of firms.

11
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:40,460
This equation says that the more firms the better.

12
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:48,700
This firm says that as n goes to infinity, perfect competition, then q goes to a over b. Right?

13
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:50,300
And then you plug that in here.

14
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:53,760
Wait. If q is a over b, then p equals a minus b times a over b.

15
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:55,260
And so p is 0.

16
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:56,540
Right? So see?

17
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:01,180
P equals a minus b times a over b.

18
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:03,200
Cancel. Cancel.

19
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:05,940
Zero. All right.

20
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:06,940
I'm just going to give up on that.

21
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:08,020
All right.

22
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:10,760
Okay. All right.

23
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:16,140
So, and then if we are the further, and on the other hand, of course,

24
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:21,540
this means that if n is 1, if n is 1, then this just gets us the monopoly result.

25
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:26,280
So n is 1, then we have q is going to be equal to a over 2b.

26
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:29,440
And if you actually remember the way that the monopoly model works,

27
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:33,760
yes, so the monopoly model with linear demand, you get the monopoly output is just equal

28
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:35,060
to half the competitive output.

29
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:36,640
All right.

30
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,720
And then anything in between 1 and infinity gives you an intermediate result.

31
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:42,400
So the more firms the better.

32
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:44,720
The more firms the better.

33
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,720
Okay. Right.

34
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:53,180
And notice, in this model, is there any collusion of any kind?

35
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:54,160
Are any firms colluding?

36
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:57,000
Or is any firm worrying about any other firm's profits?

37
00:01:57,420 --> 00:01:58,740
Nope. Right?

38
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:03,920
So we wind up getting a result that looks like a monopoly result,

39
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,960
like an intermediate monopoly result, even though there's no collusion of any kind.

40
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:08,160
All right.

41
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:11,240
So each firm is maximizing individual profits.

42
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:13,200
Each firm thinks only of itself.

43
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:16,080
But we still get a suboptimal outcome.

44
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:19,860
And remember, here we've got a really extreme assumption here.

45
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:23,460
Marginal cost is zero, and still firms are charging positive prices.

46
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:30,880
Right? So this is a, even the extreme case, there is totally pointless scarcity caused

47
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:33,840
by the finitude of firms.

48
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,580
The finitude of firms is impoverishing the world here.

49
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:40,000
Okay. All right.

50
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:42,940
So this model, so this result, I think,

51
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:44,940
explains why the current model is so much more popular.

52
00:02:44,940 --> 00:02:46,860
It's not because people say, well, if you look at the world,

53
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:48,620
firms are generally setting quantities, not prices.

54
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:51,480
That, in fact, seems wrong most of the time.

55
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:55,220
There are some markets where firms setting quantities sounds okay,

56
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:57,500
like oil production, for example.

57
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:02,300
But most markets, it sure doesn't look like firms are setting quantities

58
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,140
and then dumping it on a market with an auctioneer.

59
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:07,880
Instead, in most industries, it looks like firms are putting price tags on things.

60
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:12,000
So in terms of the realism of the assumptions, Bertrand seems better.

61
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,680
But the Bertrand outcome implies a very pro-market outcome,

62
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:17,280
which most economists don't like.

63
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:22,960
Whereas the Cournot model seems to apply in much more market skeptical outcome,

64
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:26,080
much more pessimistic outcome for markets.

65
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:30,480
And so, or I think many would say a more realistic outcome,

66
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:32,780
and therefore is more popular.

67
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:33,440
Yeah?

68
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:37,980
Is putting a price tag on something the same thing as setting a price?

69
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:39,100
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

70
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:40,520
Sure. I mean, that's cool.

71
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:43,620
I mean, it's one way to set a price.

72
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:46,700
You can also put, you don't have to have actual stickers.

73
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:48,060
You can also have a sign.

74
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:49,180
Or you can maybe.

75
00:03:49,180 --> 00:03:52,580
With the price of like a million dollars on a piece of, on a suitcase.

76
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,780
Yeah. But no one's going to buy it at a million dollars.

77
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:57,680
Yes, but you know, right, right.

78
00:03:57,880 --> 00:03:58,500
That's true.

79
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:04,140
Right. But, you know, but again, like the Bertrand model just says that firms state their prices

80
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:05,200
and that determines what happens.

81
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:08,140
Cournot model says that firms pick quantities and that determines what happens.

82
00:04:08,140 --> 00:04:10,900
So yeah, like even putting it on realistic price sounds

83
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,500
like what would happen in the Cournot model.

84
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:14,020
Although not in equilibrium.

85
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:17,460
In equilibrium they would say, well, I can't sell it for a million, so that's what I want.

86
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:18,360
All right.

87
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:23,140
Now, so far so good.

88
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:29,480
But funny thing about Cournot model is that if you actually play around with it more,

89
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:31,120
you'll realize this is all trickery.

90
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:35,800
And the Cournot model actually also applies the end outcome essentially the same

91
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:36,600
as the Bertrand outcome.

92
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:42,900
How so? Well, so in the Cournot model the number of firms is just given.

93
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:44,660
Right. There's just n firms and that's it.

94
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:49,640
But since we have a constant marginal cost model here,

95
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:52,880
there's no reason why firms shouldn't be able to split into more firms.

96
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:58,000
So no reason why one of these Cournot firms should not be able to just become two firms.

97
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:04,260
Why not? So if you want to show the Cournot model is internally consistent,

98
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:07,800
it seems like you want to show that the firms wouldn't want to split.

99
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:12,180
And that's unfortunate because if you check you will find out that firms

100
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:15,500
that are Cournot will always want to split for all n greater than 1.

101
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:17,280
All right.

102
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:19,380
So I think I have you prove this on the homework.

103
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,560
But here is the basic idea.

104
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:28,300
So is that if you go and calculate Cournot profit of n. This is Cournot profit

105
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:30,380
of one firm when there are n firms.

106
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:37,520
It turns out that this is always less than two times the Cournot profit with n plus 1 firms

107
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:39,240
for all n greater than 1.

108
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:41,500
Yeah. So if you're a monopolist you don't want to split.

109
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:44,900
If you're a monopolist you don't want to split into two firms and compete with yourself.

110
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:51,400
But if there are two firms, each of those firms would actually make more money

111
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:56,200
if it split into two firms, which of course raises the number of firms in the industry.

112
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:59,560
And as you might guess when you raise the number of firms in the industry,

113
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:02,620
what happens to the profits of each firm?

114
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:04,100
They go down.

115
00:06:04,580 --> 00:06:07,300
But if splitting lets you double your profits,

116
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:12,180
double the smaller profits is always greater than the initial profits.

117
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:13,600
All right.

118
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:17,560
So what this means is that given these assumptions,

119
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:19,580
infinite n would arise automatically.

120
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:25,760
And so you still wind up getting the perfectly competitive result, strangely enough.

121
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:27,860
All right.

122
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:33,280
Now, if you want to, if you tweak the model more you may say, well, yeah, well, of course.

123
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:40,200
Of course there's a problem with this and we'll just handle it the obvious way.

124
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:42,020
Let's add in a fixed cost.

125
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:45,480
So in that case we'll add in a fixed cost.

126
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:47,260
So there's one big problem here.

127
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:53,360
As soon as you add in a fixed cost is it now still true that infinite n is good?

128
00:06:53,360 --> 00:07:00,860
If every firm has to pay a fixed cost of k, is infinite n good for society?

129
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:05,160
No. No, because that would require an infinite cost, an infinite fixed cost.

130
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:07,840
It would be, you know, say, in fact, you know,

131
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:10,560
the fixed costs are equal to n times k, right?

132
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:11,880
So where k is the fixed cost.

133
00:07:12,340 --> 00:07:15,760
So as soon as you say that, then once again you have to give up the idea

134
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:17,540
that more firms is always better for society.

135
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:24,740
Right? And actually I think Mankiew had a paper on this where they proved, strangely,

136
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:30,420
that if there's a fixed cost, so if the cost function is, you know,

137
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:36,860
if there's a total cost, you know, k plus b times marginal cost.

138
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:42,580
If firms have a cost function like that and then you allow for entry, so firms just enter

139
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:49,420
until price equals average cost, they actually prove that Cournot combination isn't even second,

140
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:51,780
that this Cournot combination isn't even second best efficient.

141
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:54,440
So in other words, they prove under these assumptions

142
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:56,960
that there are too many firms rather than too few.

143
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:06,380
Which, again, is a huge disappointment for the great 1970s Ralph Nader trust-busting agenda,

144
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:09,720
which, again, is not talked about very much, although it's often,

145
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:14,020
although textbooks are often still written as if this, written to apply

146
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:16,980
that this makes perfect sense, these are the retro ones.

147
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:19,860
So, which is kind of annoying.

148
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:21,320
Let's see.

149
00:08:21,320 --> 00:08:24,700
So yeah, I remember I was once a referee at a textbook where it said these things

